# Where your handheld is and how to reach it.
#
#   cp device.env.example device.env
#
# device.env is gitignored. It describes your network, so it must never be
# committed -- which is also why nothing in scripts/ carries a default
# address, password or host key.
#
# scripts/setup-device.sh will write this file for you and check the result.
#
# QUOTE ANY VALUE CONTAINING A SPACE. This file is read with `.`, so it is
# shell: an unquoted  DEVICE_PLINK=/c/Program Files/PuTTY/plink.exe  parses as
# a command prefix assignment and the setting silently does not apply.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Required
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Hostname or address of the handheld. Find it in EmulationStation under
# Main Menu -> Network Settings, or from your router's client list.
#
# The stock firmware uses DHCP, so this address will move. A static lease on
# the router is the fix; otherwise expect to edit this line now and then.
DEVICE_HOST=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Login
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The stock firmware has one account.
DEVICE_USER=root

# How to connect: auto, ssh or plink.
#   auto   plink when DEVICE_PW is set and plink is installed, else ssh.
#   ssh    OpenSSH. Needs key authentication -- it cannot take a password
#          non-interactively, and these scripts never prompt.
#   plink  PuTTY's plink, which can. Usual choice on Windows.
DEVICE_TRANSPORT=auto

# Password, used only by plink. Batocera's documented default is "linux";
# change it on the device and put the new one here.
#
# This file is gitignored, but a password in a file on disk is still a
# password on disk. Prefer DEVICE_KEY below.
#DEVICE_PW=

# Private key, used by ssh (and accepted by plink in .ppk form). Install the
# public half on the device once:
#
#   ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -f ~/.ssh/rg353
#   ssh-copy-id -i ~/.ssh/rg353 root@<your device>
#
#DEVICE_KEY=

# The device's SSH host key fingerprint, pinned so that a DHCP address change
# cannot quietly point these scripts at somebody else's machine. plink prints
# it the first time you connect interactively; ssh keeps its own record in
# known_hosts and ignores this.
#DEVICE_HOSTKEY=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Tools
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Full path if plink is not on PATH. Quoted, because of the space:
#DEVICE_PLINK="/c/Program Files/PuTTY/plink.exe"

# Extra options for ssh, e.g. a jump host or a non-standard port.
#DEVICE_SSH_OPTS="-p 22"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Optional
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The address the DEVICE should use to reach this machine, for the HTTP
# transfer scripts/push.sh uses. Detected automatically; set it when this
# machine has several interfaces (VPN, WSL, Docker) and the wrong one wins.
#HOST_IP=

# The handheld's gamepad event node. Stock firmware puts it here.
#PAD=/dev/input/event3
